Yes.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:07.960
When was this, did they video it?

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:12.960
There is video of the runaway train
but not of this actual moment.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.320
-That's a real… that’s a massive oversight.
-Is it the 70s?

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:16.720
2001.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.480
Yeah, massive oversight,
get a helicopter over this, you've got rights.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.280
So an engineer with 31 years of service.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:24.640
Nice, is it his last day before retirement?

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.680
I think it was in the film,
it actually was in the film.

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:28.160
Oh, of course it f***ing was.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:30.920
And a rookie conductor with one year's
experience.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:36.100
"But it'll never work!" "Listen to me kid, I am,"
looks down imaginary camera,

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:37.160
"the engineer."

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:40.120
Looks at picture of dead wife.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.120
I watched that film thinking it was so formulaic,
but it actually happened like that.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.000
Yes, then the… the train master…

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:52.440
Oh! "Behold!"

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.560
"The train master."

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:58.080
Stood in front of it on the tracks and said,
"Halt."

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:00.320
Is the train master Brunel?

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:05.640
No, it's Topham Hatt just with
his little straight arms.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:08.960
"You have been a very naughty engine."

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:13.960
Suddenly then they listen and Ringo has suddenly
appeared in the back of the train to narrate this s***.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.280
Wait a minute, did the young engineer
try and go first,

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:19.680
and the old engineer said, "no, you've got
too much to live for" at the last second?

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.600
Pull him out of the way and do it himself.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.480
Yeah, train master, John Horsfeld,
ran alongside the train.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:25.320
Ran?

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.680
They've got it down to like 11 miles an hour
now, so he sprints along…

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.280
Okay, fifty mile an hour.
-Climbs aboard.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.120
-That's still a quick run, that.
-And shuts down the engine, yes.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:36.200
They have another train upfront to…
ready to try and…

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:37.440
Ram it.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:39.680
Not quite ram it, but, you know,
slow it down by braking.

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:42.280
-Ram it.
-Ram it, fine, yes.

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:45.980
What happened to the engineer who first
made the mistake, way way back?

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:48.480
So it's now stopped, they've recovered it,
it's going back to the yard.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:50.400
The engineer at the start, what happened to him?

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:54.400
He was made to lie down on the rails
while they backed it up.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:55.320
Ooh!

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:59.800
Did he have to walk all the way
to pick it back up again?

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:05.280
It’s actually a bit harder to work out because
they never released the name of the engineer.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.200
Yeah, I don't think I would either.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:11.920
Because in aviation, you don't do that,
you don't go for blame, you go for,

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:15.960
let's work out what happened and then work out
how to make it not happen again.

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:19.200
-Yes.
-And then you get at that rather than it's …

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.600
it's a blameless culture,
whatever they call it.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.440
-So then things get reported.
-Yeah, you've actually…

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:27.920
Because in the end it's always systems or technology
that's eventually caused the problem.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:30.040
And if not, you think you're going to get
blamed for something,

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:32.480
you're not going to report that something
happened, then you don't get good safety.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:34.360
Yeah, it's called a 'just culture'

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:37.440
and you've literally answered
several of the questions I was about to ask.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.200
-Have two points.
-Yeah!

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:41.800
Alright, so what's happened to the engine
in the end?

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:45.640
Has it been destroyed as a warning to others?

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:48.800
Is it in a retirement home?
What are they called? Museum?

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:50.640
Those are two very different things.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:53.360
A retirement home for objects is a museum.

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:55.000
Okay. Okay, right yeah.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.200
He's not wrong.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:58.320
But neither is he right.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:01.000
But that's…
that doesn't work the other way around.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.320
Like a retirement home
is not a museum of people.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:05.240
No.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.280
Several railway museums tried to buy it
but officials replied,

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.600
they "did not feel it worthy of preservation."

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:13.000
-Yes, because…
-Please forget this.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.880
The train would tell the other trains
how to run away.

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:16.920
Oh.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.080
Dangerous knowledge.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:22.120
And it was torn down and rebuilt
as part of a regular rebuild process.

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:25.520
So it is now...
bits of it are now in other places.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.160
It got hung, drawn and quartered.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:31.440
I am... yeah, basically, yeah, that's pretty much
what hung, drawing and quartering was.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:34.680
I've got the idea now, of all these bits from
other trains and these bits are sentient

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:36.920
and suddenly at some point,
and here's the second film...

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.520
Lots of trains run away.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:42.760
I'm getting a sense this second one's
written by Stephen King.

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:46.040
Ideally me, but, yeah,
alright, I'll sell the rights.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.240
So at the end of the show, congratulations,
Gary, you win this one.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.680
You win the rights
to your screenplay being produced.

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:56.600
No, sorry.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.840
You win a shirt that exposes your midriff
sponsored by a series of Korean boy bands.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:04.560
It's a K-pop crop top.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:08.720
With that we say thank you to Chris Joel,

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:10.680
to Gary Brannan,

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:11.680
to Matt Gray.

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:13.680
I've been Tom Scott,
and we'll see you next time.

